# The HTML shell must always be revalidated. Pages otherwise serves index.html
# as the SPA fallback for an absent hashed module; caching that HTML under the
# module URL poisons Chrome's module cache with the wrong MIME type across
# subsequent deployments. That hazard is permanent — `no-store` on the shell
# stays.
#
# Clear-Site-Data is GONE, and the reason is worth keeping. It was a
# one-release migration for the 0.0.774 recovery deploy (2026-08-05) and was
# never removed, so for thirteen days every visitor paid for it twice:
#
#   · ~1.5s of blocked navigation while Chrome purged the origin's cache,
#     measured as responseStart→responseEnd on a 3.9KB document that curl
#     fetches end-to-end in under 300ms. /404.html, same origin and same
#     no-store but without the header, measures 0ms.
#   · every repeat visit re-downloaded the whole site — 0 of 44 same-origin
#     requests served from cache, 1.6MB, every time.
#
# If a future recovery needs it again: add it, ship one release, take it out
# the same week. It is not a header a site can wear.
/
  Cache-Control: no-store

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-store

# These routes are prerendered because the real top-level 404 deliberately
# disables Pages' implicit SPA fallback. Match the extensionless request URL,
# not the .html file Pages resolves internally.
/privacy
  Cache-Control: no-store

/manifesto
  Cache-Control: no-store

/terms
  Cache-Control: no-store

/404.html
  Cache-Control: no-store

# The worker tombstones stay. The retirement script that used to run in the
# HTML shell is gone — it cost every visitor a getRegistrations() call and
# could force a whole second navigation — but these files cost nothing to
# anyone who does not already have a stale registration pointing at them, and
# they are the only remaining path that retires one. A visitor who has not
# been here since before 2026-08-05 still needs it.
/service_worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-store
  Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8

/service-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-store
  Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-store
  Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8

# Content-hashed filenames. A given URL's bytes can never change, so there is
# nothing to revalidate — the previous `max-age=0, must-revalidate` bought a
# round-trip per chunk for no possible benefit, across ~38 chunks per load.
# (Note the deployed site was answering `max-age=14400` here regardless, which
# is Cloudflare Pages' own default; confirm this rule actually lands after the
# next publish rather than assuming it did.)
/_app/immutable/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
